---
/**
 * /solvers/ — the three solver families, off the landing page.
 *
 * These moved here because presenting them as headline capabilities invites a
 * comparison this project loses and does not want: mesh scale, turbulence
 * closures, BC libraries, ecosystem maturity. The honest job they do is
 * narrower and stronger — they are why the coupled loop closes without an
 * external dependency.
 *
 * Content is copied from `components/home/SolverStack.astro`, which stays on
 * the landing page until the rework's cleanup phase retires it. Duplication is
 * deliberate and temporary: nothing loses a destination mid-flight.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';

const title = 'Solvers — DeepCausality CFD';
const description =
  'Three solver families behind one CfdFlow language: a DEC-native incompressible discretization, quantized tensor-train compressible marchers, and gridless fitted closures. What each is for, and what each does not do.';

const families = [
  {
    key: 'DEC',
    label: 'Calculus',
    title: 'DEC-native Navier–Stokes',
    forWhat: 'Incompressible flow on a fixed mesh.',
    body:
      'Velocity lives as an edge 1-form on a discrete exterior calculus. Each step marches the Leray-projected rate, so the field is divergence-free at every step. The SolenoidalField type-state carries that: there is no public constructor, every constructing path is a projection, and the type implements no arithmetic, so two projected fields cannot be added into an unprojected one.',
    caveat:
      'Two wall-bounded escape hatches are public. constrain_edges and with_lift re-wrap a modified tensor without re-projecting, because the solver re-enters them on the output of a projection that already pinned those edges. Off that path the caller carries the invariant.',
    evidence: [
      { text: 'Ghia cavity Re 1000', href: '/validation/#dec-cavity' },
      { text: 'Cylinder Re 100', href: '/validation/#dec-cylinder' },
      { text: 'Graded MMS, order 2.00', href: '/validation/#dec-graded-mms' },
    ],
  },
  {
    key: 'QTT',
    label: 'Compression',
    title: 'Quantized tensor-train marchers',
    forWhat: 'Compressible Euler, 1-D through 3-D, including a body-fitted variant.',
    body:
      'A 2^L grid stores order χ²·L: logarithmic in point count for a bounded bond dimension χ, with sharp structure paid for in χ. Whether χ stays bounded is the question the rank studies answered, and they found the driver to be coordinate alignment rather than sharpness. The compressible carrier acts on that with a shock-fitted inflow strip, which imposes the exact Rankine–Hugoniot state as the boundary of the marched layer, so the shock is never captured at all.',
    caveat:
      'Storage is not runtime. On the incompressible immersed harness, per-step wall clock rose far faster than χ²·L while the achieved bond stayed flat, so a non-compression bottleneck dominates that path.',
    evidence: [
      { text: 'Sod, L1 ≤ 0.027', href: '/validation/#qtt-sod' },
      { text: 'RAM-C II anchor', href: '/validation/#qtt-ramc' },
      { text: 'Alignment is the rank driver', href: '/boundaries/#alignment' },
    ],
  },
  {
    key: 'Analytic',
    label: 'Pointwise',
    title: 'Fitted closures',
    forWhat: 'Gridless stagnation-line and relaxation problems.',
    body:
      'Exact Rankine–Hugoniot jumps, Park two-temperature relaxation, the finite-rate ionization network, and pointwise Navier–Stokes regime evaluators with their causal-effect wrappers. A stagnation line with a fitted shock runs entirely on these, with no grid.',
    caveat: null,
    evidence: [
      { text: 'MMS to machine ε', href: '/validation/#mms-tgv' },
      { text: 'Envelope placard example', href: '/examples/flight-envelope-placard/' },
    ],
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/solvers/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <article class="page spage">
    <header class="shead">
      <p class="eyebrow">Solvers</p>
      <h1>Three solvers, one job</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        These exist so the coupled loop can close without an external
        dependency. They are not offered as a replacement for the solver you
        already run.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="handoff panel corner-brackets">
      <h2>Where the line sits</h2>
      <p class="detail">
        Meshing is your mesher's job — this reads no mesh format, and geometry
        is declared in code on structured Cartesian grids with aperture-resolved
        cut cells. Converged flow at industrial scale, turbulence closures and
        certification stay with your solver. What happens at the seams between
        domains, under alternatives, with the record of why, is what this is
        for.
      </p>
      <p class="detail">
        All three families sit behind the same <code>CfdFlow</code> language and
        the same scalar type, so a program picks by problem: the DEC solver for
        an incompressible cavity, the QTT marcher for a reentry layer, a fitted
        closure for the stagnation line.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="stack">
      {families.map((f, i) => (
        <article class="layer" style={`--i:${i}`}>
          <div class="layer-label">
            <span class="lk">{f.label}</span>
            <span class="lv">{f.key}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="layer-body">
            <h3>{f.title}</h3>
            <p class="for-what">{f.forWhat}</p>
            <p class="detail">{f.body}</p>
            {f.caveat && <p class="caveat">{f.caveat}</p>}
            <div class="ev-row">
              {f.evidence.map((e) => (
                <a class="chip chip-mono" href={e.href}>
                  {e.text}<span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <section class="tail">
      <p class="more">
        <a href="/solvers/precision/">Precision as a parameter →</a>
        <a href="/blueprints/pick-a-solver/">Blueprint: pick a solver</a>
        <a href="/validation/">Validation status</a>
        <a href="/boundaries/">Measured boundaries</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .spage { max-width: var(--w-panel); }
  .shead { margin-bottom: var(--space-7); }
  .shead h1 { margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-4); }
  .shead .lede { color: var(--fg-1); max-width: var(--measure-lede); margin: 0; }

  .handoff { padding: var(--space-5); margin-bottom: var(--space-7); }
  .handoff h2 { font-size: var(--t-h4); margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); }

  .stack { border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); }

  .layer {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    padding: var(--space-5) var(--space-4);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1);
    transition: background-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .layer { grid-template-columns: var(--w-label) 1fr; gap: var(--space-5); }
  }
  /* The tick grows 18px → 28px on hover (DESIGN.md §8.4). Implemented as
     scaleY rather than an animated `height`, which §13.10 bans: 28/18 = 1.556
     on a fixed 18px base gives the same result on the compositor. */
  .layer::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 50%;
    width: 3px;
    height: 18px;
    transform: translateY(-50%) scaleY(1);
    transform-origin: center;
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 55%, transparent);
    transition: transform var(--dur-med) var(--ease-out),
                background-color var(--dur-med) var(--ease-out);
  }
  .layer:hover { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 5%, transparent); }
  .layer:hover::before { transform: translateY(-50%) scaleY(1.556); background: var(--accent); }

  .layer-label { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-1); }
  .lk {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }
  .lv {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-mono-sm);
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .layer-body h3 { margin: 0 0 var(--space-2); }
  .for-what { color: var(--fg-0); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); }
  .detail { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); }
  .detail:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .caveat {
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-size: var(--t-body-sm);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line-2);
  }
  .ev-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-2); }

  .tail { padding-top: var(--space-5); }
  .more {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--t-body-sm);
  }
</style>
